---
permalink: /docs/search-index.json
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
{#- The search index, built from the pages Eleventy just rendered rather than from
    the Markdown. That is the point: the index cannot describe a page the site does
    not actually serve, and the heading ids it links to are the ids that
    markdown-it-anchor emitted, not a second guess at them.

    It sits at the ROOT, beside sitemap.njk, and not in docs/ — docs/docs.json sets
    templateEngineOverride:"md" for everything in that directory, which would render
    this file as Markdown and emit a JSON file full of <p> tags. Same reason
    sitemap.njk is not in a content directory.

    It is NOT loaded with the page. docs.js fetches it on the first focus of the
    search box, so a reader who never searches never pays for it.

    Keys are one letter because this file ships to the browser: u=url, t=title,
    s=section, h=headings [{i:id, t:text}], x=text. -#}
[
{%- for p in collections.docs %}
  {%- set crumbs = p.url | crumbs(collections.docs) %}
  {%- set sect = [] %}
  {%- for c in crumbs %}{% if not loop.last and c.url != "/docs/" %}{% set _ = sect.push(c.title) %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
  {%- set hs = p.templateContent | headings %}
  {
    "u": {{ p.url | dump | safe }},
    "t": {{ p.data.title | dump | safe }},
    "s": {{ sect.join(" / ") | dump | safe }},
    "h": [{% for h in hs %}{"i":{{ h.id | dump | safe }},"t":{{ h.text | dump | safe }}}{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}{% endfor %}],
    "x": {{ p.templateContent | searchText | dump | safe }}
  }{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}
{%- endfor %}
]
